1
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:21,000
If Nancy doesn't wake up screaming, she won't wake up at all.

2
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:36,000
A Nightmare on Elm Street is a horror film directed by Wes Craven.

3
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:51,000
Teenagers in a small town are dropping like flies, apparently in
the grip of mass hysteria causing their suicides.

4
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:06,000
A cop's daughter, Nancy Thompson, traces the cause to child molester Fred
Krueger, who was burned alive by angry parents many years before.

5
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:21,000
Krueger has now come back in the dreams of his killers'
children, claiming their lives as his revenge.

6
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:36,000
Nancy and her boyfriend, Glen, must devise a plan to lure the monster
out of the realm of nightmares and into the real world...

7
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:51,000
Nancy Thompson, played by Heather Langenkamp.

8
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:06,000
Freddy Krueger, played by Robert Englund.

9
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:21,000
Lieutenant Thompson, played by John Saxon.

10
00:32:30,000 --> 00:32:36,000
Marge Thompson, played by Ronee Blakley.

11
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:51,000
Glen Lantz, played by Johnny Depp.

12
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:06,000
Rod Lane, played by Jsu Garcia.

13
00:42:15,000 --> 00:42:21,000
A Nightmare on Elm Street is a 1984 American supernatural slasher film
written and directed by Wes Craven and produced by Robert Shaye.

14
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:36,000
It is the first installment in the A Nightmare on Elm Street franchise and stars Heather Langenkamp, John
Saxon, Ronee Blakley, Robert Englund as Freddy Krueger, and Johnny Depp in his film debut.

15
00:48:45,000 --> 00:48:51,000
The film's plot concerns a group of teenagers who are targeted by Krueger, an undead child killer who murders
teenagers through their dreams, as retribution against their parents who burned him alive.

16
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:06,000
Craven filmed A Nightmare on Elm Street in Los Angeles
on an estimated budget of $1.1 million.

17
00:55:15,000 --> 00:55:21,000
It was one of the first films produced by New Line Cinema, who by that point mostly distributed films, leading the company
to become a successful mini-major film studio and earning it the nickname "The House that Freddy Built".

18
00:58:30,000 --> 00:58:36,000
The film is credited with carrying on many tropes found in low-budget horror films of the 1970s and 1980s, originating in John Carpenter's Halloween (1978),
including the morality play that revolves around sexual promiscuity in teenagers resulting in their eventual death, leading to the term "slasher film".

19
01:01:45,000 --> 01:01:51,000
Critics and film historians state that the film's premise is the struggle to define the distinction
between dreams and reality, manifested by the lives and dreams of the teens in the film.

20
01:05:00,000 --> 01:05:06,000
Later critics praise the film's ability to transgress "the boundaries between
the imaginary and real", toying with audience perceptions.

21
01:08:15,000 --> 01:08:21,000
A Nightmare on Elm Street was released on November 9,
1984, and grossed $57.1 million worldwide.

22
01:11:30,000 --> 01:11:36,000
The film received critical acclaim upon its release, and has since been
considered to be one of the greatest horror films ever made.

23
01:14:45,000 --> 01:14:51,000
In 2021, A Nightmare on Elm Street was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry
by the Library of Congress as being "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

24
01:18:00,000 --> 01:18:06,000
The film spawned a franchise consisting of six sequels, a television series, a crossover with
Friday the 13th, various other merchandise, and a remake of the same name.

25
01:21:15,000 --> 01:21:21,000
The film was followed by A Nightmare on Elm Street 2: Freddy's Revenge (1985).

26
01:24:30,000 --> 01:24:36,000
The film explores themes of dreams, nightmare, psychopath, sleep, child murder.

27
01:27:45,000 --> 01:27:51,000
The tone is dark and intense.
